# Static-analysis profile for the metaRelayer store-and-forward bot.
#
# This file is part of the coding standard: it defines which checks are enforced
# and records every deliberate deviation with its justification. Run it with
#      make analyze
#
# A deviation is only acceptable when the rationale is written down here.
---
Checks: >
  -*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  bugprone-*,
  cert-*,
  performance-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-narrowing-conversions,
  -cert-err33-c,
  -clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling

WarningsAsErrors: 'clang-analyzer-*,cert-*'

CheckOptions:
  # Longest identifier accepted before readability checks complain.
  readability-identifier-length.MinimumVariableNameLength: 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documented deviations
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#   The store-and-forward APIs take several integer parameters of the same
#   width, for example
#       mq_enqueue(int friendnum, time_t now, const char *payload, size_t len)
#       msg_database_add(int friend_num, int msg_sn, time_t datetime, ...)
#   These signatures are deliberate mirror images of the on-the-wire telegram
#   fields. Every call site is covered by unit tests that would fail if two
#   arguments were transposed (see tests/test_msg_queue.c and
#   tests/test_msg_database.c), so the advisory check is not enforced.
#
# bugprone-narrowing-conversions
#   toxcore and sqlite3 exchange values through 8/16/32 bit integer types.
#   Conversions are explicit at the boundary (see tox_transport() in toxbot.c
#   and the sqlite3_bind_* calls in msg_database.c) and verified by tests.
#
# cert-err34-c
#   Enforced. Every integer that used to come from atoi()/atol() now goes through
#   parse_int_range() in misc.c, which checks the entire string, tests errno for
#   overflow and range-checks the result; the strtol() calls in telegram.c check
#   their end pointers and range-check the parsed serial. The check is enabled so
#   that a new unchecked conversion cannot be introduced.
#
# cert-err33-c
#   Disabled. The check reports a discarded return value, and how it does so
#   changed between clang-tidy versions: 18 reports every ignored snprintf()
#   (glibc declares the fortified snprintf() with warn_unused_result), while 21
#   reports none of the same call sites. A gate whose verdict depends on the
#   tool version is worse than no gate: the same commit passes locally and fails
#   in CI, which teaches people to ignore it.
#   The call sites where a discarded result could hide a real problem are already
#   checked explicitly: log.c tests the result of vsnprintf(), mq_persist.c tests
#   every fwrite/fflush/fsync/fclose and reports the failure, and the bounded
#   string copies go through copy_tox_str() and unquote_str(). What remains is
#   formatting for operator-facing text, where truncation is acceptable and there
#   is no action to take. Auditing those individually is recorded as remaining
#   work rather than done by machine.
#
# clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling
#   The check demands the C11 Annex K "_s" functions (fprintf_s, sscanf_s, ...).
#   Annex K is optional, is not implemented by glibc or the macOS libc, and is
#   not portable to the platforms this relay targets. The bounded C11 functions
#   that are available (snprintf, vsnprintf, fgets) are used throughout, and the
#   one sscanf("%2x") call has been replaced by explicit bounded decoding.
#   Enforcing this check would require non-portable code, so it is disabled
#   deliberately rather than making the code unportable.
